In TypeScript, the built-in `Map` object can be used to maintain the insertion order of key-value pairs. Although it is not explicitly called an "ordered dictionary," it serves a similar purpose by preserving the order in which elements were added.

Here's an example of how you can use a `Map` to achieve ordered storage of values:

```typescript
const orderedDict = new Map<string, any>();

orderedDict.set("key1", "value1");
orderedDict.set("key2", "value2");
orderedDict.set("key3", "value3");

for (const value of orderedDict.values()) {
  console.log(value);
}
```

The output will be:

```
value1
value2
value3
```

The values are retrieved in the same order they were inserted into the `Map`. By using `Map` instead of a plain object (`{}`), you can ensure that the insertion order is maintained.

It's important to note that the `Map` object provides additional functionality beyond an ordered dictionary, such as the ability to iterate over keys and key-value pairs.

